The Explanation:
This capture shows TLS Application Data transmitted over TCP port 443 using IPv6. The packet contains encrypted payloads that Wireshark identifies as part of a reassembled TLS segment. Because TLS encrypts all application-layer content after the handshake, the payload appears as random bytes in the hex view. The TCP segment includes an ACK, sequence numbers, and a window size, indicating normal encrypted HTTPS communication. This capture demonstrates how Wireshark displays encrypted TLS traffic and how TCP reassembly is used to reconstruct full TLS records.
This is exactly how SOC analysts document TLS traffic.
Why this capture matters for my SOC/OT training
TLS hides the content — but metadata is still useful
Even though the payload is encrypted, we can still analyze:
•	Packet size
•	Timing
•	Sequence numbers
•	Retransmissions
•	IPv6 vs IPv4
•	Server IP reputation
•	Session behavior
This is how SOC analysts detect:
•	Malware beaconing
•	Data exfiltration
•	Suspicious encrypted traffic
•	TLS anomalies
•	Failed handshakes
